    not_duplicate_of: []  # d689
    pr_ref:  # d689
    description: "## Symptom\n\nPR https://codeberg.org/iterabloom/hypergumbo/pulls/2729 was created by the\ntracker auto-sync mechanism on 2026-04-06 21:00:30 -0400. Its single commit,\na53f4d769fa835549feac85eacf6306efeb57629, contains zero file changes (`git\nshow` displays no patch at all). The parent commit, 666b84839f6a9b6310de9525-\nfff4d06fe70483e1, is already merged on dev. So the PR contributes nothing —\nit is a no-op against an already-merged base — yet it remains open in the\nforge with no CI status reported (since an empty commit appears not to\ntrigger a CI workflow run on Forgejo, which is also why\n`./scripts/ci-debug pr-status 2729` originally produced confusing output;\nthat secondary parser bug was fixed in PR #2759 / commit a06ff93c6).\n\n## What this item is *not*\n\nThis is NOT about ci-debug's NoneType handling. That bug is fixed.\n\nThis is NOT about the previously-resolved auto-sync bugs:\n- WI-japom-bimon (auto-sync merge failures when dev diverges during CI)\n- WI-lufal-gutop (failed push should not discard local ops files)\n- WI-vogod-tapop (auto-sync circuit breaker and branch safety)\n- WI-modab-kujab (batch mutation command with deferred auto-sync)\n- WI-mijib-gavir (exclude tracker ops from auto-pr PRs)\n\nNone of those address the specific failure mode here, which is:\n\"_maybe_auto_sync committed and pushed when there were no pending ops to\nsync, producing an empty commit on top of an already-merged parent.\"\n\n## Hypothesis (NOT yet verified — investigation required)\n\nMost plausible cause: a race or stale-state read in `_maybe_auto_sync`.\nTwo scenarios I have not ruled out:\n1. **Concurrent invocation race.** Two tracker mutations triggered\n   `_maybe_auto_sync` at nearly the same time. The first invocation\n   committed all pending ops and pushed successfully. The second\n   invocation then ran with the pending-ops-counter still showing\n   above the threshold (because it computed the delta from a stale\n   snapshot taken before the first invocation finished), built a\n   commit-tree from the now-empty staging area, and pushed it.\n2. **Threshold check vs. stage-files race.** The threshold check\n   (`pending_lines > 40`) and the actual `git add`/`write-tree` happen\n   non-atomically. Between the two, another process could have moved\n   all pending ops out from under us, leaving `git add` to stage\n   nothing — but the commit was created anyway because the code did\n   not check for an empty index before calling `commit-tree`.\n3. **Some third thing.** Don't anchor on the above hypotheses.\n\n## What to investigate\n\n1. **Reproduce the failure mode locally.** Try to coax `_maybe_auto_sync`\n   into producing an empty commit by:\n   - Driving two tracker mutations from parallel processes.\n   - Manually pre-emptying the ops queue between threshold-check and\n     commit.\n   - Reading the auto-sync code in `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/` and\n     identifying every place a commit could be created with an empty\n     diff.\n\n2. **Identify the exact code path.** Find the function(s) that build\n   the sync commit. Determine whether there is currently *any* check\n   that the staged tree differs from HEAD before `commit-tree` is\n   called. If not, that is at minimum a structural gap (an invariant\n   violation: 'tracker auto-sync commits must have a non-empty diff').\n   File a sub-invariant if appropriate.\n\n3. **Audit historical PRs for the same pattern.** Run a query against\n   the Forgejo API (via an approved script — extending one if needed)\n   to list tracker-sync PRs whose head commit has an empty diff. If\n   #2729 is the only one, this might be a one-off. If it has happened\n   before, this is a recurring class of bug.\n\n4. **Audit historical commits on dev for empty tracker-sync commits.**\n   `git log --pretty=format:'%H %s' dev | grep '^.* tracker: sync' |\n   while read sha _; do\n     if [ -z \\\"\\$(git show --pretty='' --name-only \\$sha)\\\" ]; then\n       echo \\\"empty: \\$sha\\\"\n     fi\n   done`\n   This gives a base rate. If the rate is non-zero, the bug  # d689
      has been\n   happening silently before too — most empty commits would land on\n   dev without anyone noticing.\n\n## What the fix should look like\n\nLikely structural: the auto-sync code should refuse to create a commit\nwhen the working tree is identical to HEAD. Pseudocode:\n\n    staged_tree = write_tree()\n    head_tree = parse_tree(HEAD)\n    if staged_tree == head_tree:\n        log('no pending ops to sync, skipping commit')\n        return\n    commit = commit_tree(staged_tree, parent=HEAD, message=...)\n\nThis is a one-line guard, but it must be placed *after* all the staging\nlogic runs, not before — because the threshold check is a heuristic and\nthe only ground truth is the actual git tree.\n\nAlso consider: should the auto-sync mechanism use file-locking or a\nnamed pidfile to prevent concurrent invocations from racing? If\ninvestigation reveals the race in scenario (1), this might be the\nbetter fix — preventing the race at its source rather than catching\nit at the empty-commit boundary.\n\n## Testing requirements\n\nTests must run in `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/tests/`. Required:\n- Unit test: `_maybe_auto_sync` with no pending ops → no commit created,\n  no push attempted, no PR opened.\n- Unit test: empty staged tree → guard fires, function returns without\n  commit_tree being called.\n- Concurrency test (if scenario 1 is the cause): two parallel calls\n  to `_maybe_auto_sync` with overlapping ops → exactly one commit is\n  created, never zero, never two.\n- Property test: invariant 'every tracker-sync commit has at least\n  one file changed in its diff vs. parent'.\n\n100% coverage required per AGENTS.md. If the fix introduces any\ndefensive code paths that can't be exercised by a real test, mark\nthem `# pragma: no cover` with a brief comment justifying it.\n\n## Manual cleanup\n\nPR #2729 itself should be closed before this work begins, since it\nwill sit open until then. (I asked the user to do this manually\nbecause no approved script exposes a 'close PR' subcommand — see\nalso the related observation that the CI Interaction Policy might\nbenefit from a small extension here.)\n\n## Priority justification\n\nP4 (lowest). Empty PRs are noise, not damage: they don't break dev,\ndon't merge anything wrong, and are easy to close manually. The cost\nof leaving the bug unfixed is occasional clutter in the PR queue and\noccasional confusion (like the ci-debug parse error this triggered).\nWorth fixing, but nothing urgent depends on it."  # d689
    fields: {}  # d689
